(Eventual $\varepsilon$-steadiness). A sequence ${a_n}$ is $eventually$ $\varepsilon$-steady iff there exists an $N \geq 0$ such that ${a_n}_{n=N}$ is $\varepsilon$-steady. In other words, 
\begin{align*}
{a_n} \text{ is eventually } \varepsilon\text{-steady} &if and only if \tex N \geq 0 \text{ such that } a_i \simeq a_j \forall \text{ } i, j \geq N \\
&if and only if \tex N \geq 0 \text{ such that } d(a_i, a_j) \leq \varepsilon \cdot a \forall \text{ } i, j \geq N \\
\end{align*}

